Declare _lseek_r at the width the libc was built with

_lseek_r is called by the prebuilt newlib libc -- stdio.o carries the seek logic
that fseek() and fseeko() end up in -- and by the ROM's libc stub table. Both were
built when off_t was a 32-bit long, and both still pass 32 bits. The declaration
and the definition here were written in terms of off_t, which is right while off_t
is 32 bits and wrong as soon as a project widens it: the callers would pass 32 bits
where 64 are read, and every fseek() would land somewhere else.

That is not hypothetical. With off_t widened to 64 bits, a 12 KB SFTP upload fails
with "seek failed", because the write path seeks to the end of the file before
each chunk and lands past it.

So _lseek_r keeps the width it has always had -- a 32-bit boundary, widening
inside it -- and stops being an alias for esp_vfs_lseek, whose signature follows
off_t and should. lseek(), pread() and pwrite() are untouched and stay 64-bit,
which is where a file over 4 GiB is actually reached. stdio's own seeking stays
limited to 4 GiB; that is a property of the libc this is linked against, not of
this change.

Two notes for a maintainer:

- The declaration in reent_syscalls.c has to agree with the definition here: it is
the weak fallback for builds without the VFS, so a mismatch is a compile error in
those builds only.

- A project that widens off_t also has to pin the declaration in the toolchain's
reent.h, which says "extern _off_t _lseek_r (struct _reent *, int, _off_t, int)"
and follows the type by construction. That file is not in this repository; it is
correct for every project as it stands, and pinning it to int is harmless to one
that never widens anything. The better fix on this side is a Kconfig knob for
64-bit off_t, which would make the width explicit instead of implied -- there is
none today.

diff --git a/components/esp_libc/src/reent_syscalls.c b/components/esp_libc/src/reent_syscalls.c
